WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.340
How do you build a

2
00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.740
nondeterministic automaton from a regular

3
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.320
expression? Follow the expression’s structure

4
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.760
and combine machines for its

5
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.480
smaller parts. Our tutorial expression

6
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.820
is a b or a,

7
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.920
all under star, so every

8
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.560
input must decompose into blocks

9
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.500
a b or a.

10
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.800
Start with the atomic expression

11
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.760
a. Use one start state,

12
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.600
one final state and an

13
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.780
a-arrow between them, leaving every

14
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.200
other transition absent. The machine

15
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.700
accepts exactly the one-symbol word

16
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.700
a, not epsilon or two

17
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.360
copies of a.

18
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.780
Construct a separate two-state machine

19
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.060
for b, then concatenate the

20
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.280
two machines. Add an epsilon

21
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.920
arrow from the a-final to

22
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.160
the b-start, keeping only the

23
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.800
b-final accepting. The resulting four-state

24
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.840
machine consumes a, crosses for

25
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.780
free, then consumes b.

26
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.500
The second alternative a needs

27
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.040
its own fresh copy of

28
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.240
the atomic machine. Sharing states

29
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.680
casually could introduce unintended paths

30
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.420
between alternatives. A structural construction

31
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.840
is meant to work mechanically

32
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.780
for every expression, not merely

33
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.580
resemble the desired language in

34
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.160
this example.

35
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.680
For union, introduce a new

36
00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.580
start with epsilon arrows to

37
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.660
the two branch starts. Keep

38
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.100
both branch finals accepting, so

39
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.080
one complete accepting branch is

40
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.160
enough. A word cannot take

41
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.440
half the a b branch

42
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.800
and then jump into the

43
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.420
other branch, because no such

44
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.260
edge was added.

45
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.740
Now apply star using another

46
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.740
fresh accepting start. Link it

47
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:30.760
to the union start by

48
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.180
epsilon, and add epsilon restarts

49
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.580
from both old finals to

50
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.520
the union start. Keep the

51
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.360
old finals accepting, so a

52
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.080
completed last block need not

53
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.800
restart before the word ends.

54
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.980
Trace a b a by

55
00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.440
choosing the a b branch

56
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.500
first. After consuming those two

57
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.120
symbols, restart at the union

58
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.600
start and take the one-symbol

59
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.640
a branch. The last symbol

60
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.780
is consumed in a final

61
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.620
state, matching the factorization a

62
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.360
b followed by a.

63
00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.500
The empty word uses zero

64
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.640
repetitions and is accepted immediately

65
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.300
at the fresh start. The

66
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.680
word b has no valid

67
00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.760
first block, so every possible

68
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.760
branch fails. The word a

69
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.380
a is accepted as two

70
00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.400
a blocks, showing why choosing

71
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.960
the shortest-looking alternative greedily is

72
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.160
not the definition of the

73
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:16.480
machine.

74
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.920
The general proof is structural

75
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.960
induction. Atomic fragments recognize their

76
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.860
base languages, and each union,

77
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.540
concatenation or star construction preserves

78
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.460
the corresponding language operation. Therefore

79
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.800
the final automaton recognizes the

80
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.420
meaning of the entire expression,

81
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.400
not merely the examples we

82
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:35.740
traced.

83
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.740
Things to take note of.

84
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.660
If the question requests the

85
00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.440
inductive construction, show the intermediate

86
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.100
machines even if a smaller

87
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.180
one is obvious. Distinguish absent

88
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.920
transitions from epsilon arrows, rename

89
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.140
components to avoid collisions, and

90
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.140
preserve the accepting-state rules for

91
00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:53.900
each operator.
